  "query_preview": "..\n\nA 60-80 knot southwesterly jet stream sits over southeastern\nArizona today, associated with a deep west coast trough. In\nresponse, a moderate pressure gradient has tightened up over\nthe area and was producing sustained 15-25 mph winds with\ngusts of 35-45 mph. A slight chance of stronger wind gusts is\npossible with the downward momentum transfer of stronger winds \naloft via showers and thunderstorms into the evening. The \npressure gradient will weaken this evening, with winds at the \nsurface diminishing. The current Wind Advisory is in effect \nuntil 11 PM MST, but it may be cancelled early if winds drop off\nquickly. Shower/thunderstorm activity will be moving fairly \nquick today with the strong flow aloft, keeping any rainfall \ntotals on the lighter side (under 0.25\" for the most part) even \nwith moderate rainfall rates.\n\nWinds remain elevated and out of the southwest on Monday but a \nbit weaker than today as the upper trough remains to the west. \n15 to 20 mph sustained winds with gusts of 20-30 mph will be\ncommon from the late morning until the early evening. A few\nshowers or a thunderstorm can't be ruled out, mainly in central\nto western portions of the state. The upper trough will then\ncross the area late Monday through Tuesday, bringing 20-50\npercent chances. Winds remain breezy on Tuesday, then should\ncome down further on Wednesday. The crossing of the upper \ntrough will cool temperatures to 5-10 degrees below normal \nTuesday and Wednesday, potentially even cooler on Wednesday. \nCurrent forecast keeps high temperatures below 80 across most of\nsoutheastern Arizona on Wednesday. \n\nModels continue to advertise a pressure gradient easterly wind \nevent late this week with a cut-off low setting up to the west \nand a surface high building south through the central US to the \neast. Though a signal persists into Saturday, most guidance is \nhighlighting Friday as the more likely day for widespread winds.\nLess confidence exists in strength, though at least widespread \nadvisory levels look likely. This pattern shift should also\nbring temperatures back up a few degrees into the weekend, \nthough still forecast to be below normal for now.",
